  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nAs of 3 AM Tuesday morning, broad southwesterly midlevel flow \nremains across the central Plains with a shortwave trough lifting \nnortheast from central OK. This was producing an axis of light to \nmoderate rainfall across portions central and south-central KS. Rain \nrates have been generally less than 0.05\" and will keep most rain \ntotals less than 0.25\". As the aforementioned shortwave trough lifts \ninto MO later this morning, the axis of rain will move out of the \narea as well. The other notable weather feature ongoing is a stalled \nsurface boundary from portions of southwest KS into central KS. A \ndrastic temperature gradient is evident with Russell currently at 48 \nwith Hutchinson at 60. This stalled boundary will play a key role in \ntemperatures this afternoon. The temperature forecast today has been \nheavily weighted to short range guidance with highs expected to \nrange from the upper 50s and 60s across central KS to the mid 70s \nacross southeast KS. \n\nTransitioning into tonight the broad southwesterly midlevel flow \nwill continue across the Plains with lee trough developing across \nhigh Plains. This will promote the strengthening of a LLJ from west \nTX through much of KS. Steepening midlevel lapse rates combined with \nthe WAA should result in isolated to scattered thunderstorms late \ntonight into Wednesday morning. Elevated instability up to 500 J/kg \nshould limit any potential for strong or severe storms. A shortwave \ntrough will slide across the northern Plains Wednesday, shunting the \naforementioned surface trough towards central KS. Ahead of the \nfront, temperatures will rebound into the upper 70s to near 80. \nAdditional WAA driven thunderstorms are possible late Wednesday \nnight into Thursday morning but the best chances may stay across \nnortheast KS into northwest MO. \n\nRain chances will diminish into the daytime hours Thursday as \nshortwave ridging slides across the central Plains ahead of the next \nshortwave trough axis. This shortwave trough is posed to lift from \nthe Baja region late Thursday night into Friday. This should result \nin widespread rain showers Friday and even Saturday. \n\nMid to long range guidance continues to bring a strong midlevel \ntrough into the central Plains during the late weekend/early \nnext week time period. This could bring multiple days of \nrainfall and strong winds. Stay tuned.",
